WEBVTT

NOTE Short AI narration · track-b-short · Eve

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.284
Alpha-Gal Audio Companion. Track B.

00:00:03.404 --> 00:00:15.141
Welcome. Tick-Primed Hypersensitivity in Cosmetic and Aesthetic Procedures, by Marielle He, Jordan Kai Simmons, and Babak Azizzadeh. Screen. Substitute. Prevent.

00:00:15.261 --> 00:00:25.824
No study has quantified cosmetic or aesthetic alpha-gal reactions or assayed a product brand. The tiers are precautionary.

00:00:25.944 --> 00:00:45.896
A-G-S is an acquired I-G-E–mediated allergy to galactose-alpha-1,3-galactose in non-primate mammalian tissue and products. Tick bites can induce alpha-gal I-G-E. Diagnosis requires compatible symptoms plus alpha-gal-specific I-G-E. Sensitization alone is not A-G-S.

00:00:46.016 --> 00:01:01.860
Cosmetic and aesthetic practice uses gelatin, collagen, thrombin, grafts, implants, sutures, heparin, injectables, and excipients. Some are mammalian-derived. Composition is an audit prompt, not proof of reaction.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:17.824
The C-D-C identified 90,018 suspected U.S. cases from 2017 through 2022 and estimated 96,000 to 450,000 affected since 2010. Seropositivity is not clinical A-G-S.

00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:27.920
Route determines timing. Oral: delayed three to six hours. Parenteral: may be immediate. Implants can prolong exposure.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:40.950
Six steps: history; confirm; audit; verify; substitute source-verified hyaluronic acid, or H-A, and non-gelatin options; prepare epinephrine, with allergy input if uncertain.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:58.088
The established pathway is tick-induced alpha-gal I-G-E reacting to a later mammalian exposure; a hypothesized product-associated sensitization pathway remains unestablished. Screening can detect existing sensitization, not a product-specific reaction.

00:01:58.208 --> 00:02:28.723
Material risk. Demonstrated: gelatin hemostats. Clinical signal: porcine unfractionated heparin, or U-F-H — 4 of 17 patients, or 24 percent, with high-dose intravenous cardiac exposure. Mechanistic: mammalian matrices, no prospective cosmetic or aesthetic cohort. Theoretical: collagen P-M-M-A and catgut. No established A-G-S-specific risk: source-verified H-A, calcium hydroxylapatite, poly-L-lactic acid, or P-L-L-A, and lidocaine.

00:02:28.843 --> 00:02:38.233
Delayed filler or matrix reactions do not prove A-G-S, and alpha-gal screening will not detect them.

00:02:38.353 --> 00:03:03.586
No prospective cosmetic or aesthetic cohort, validated procedure-specific screening threshold, reaction registry, or brand-level alpha-gal assay exists. Targeted non-systematic narrative review; 19 sources; no pooling. Disclosures none. Funding none. Artificial-intelligence assistance was used for formatting, coding, and companion design; authors verified all content.

00:03:03.706 --> 00:03:16.650
This narration is decision support for licensed clinicians, not medical advice. Premedication does not reliably prevent anaphylaxis. Narrated by Marielle He. End of narration.
